    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_epidermis",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Epidermis and stomata",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Both layers and the stomata",
        "margin_note": "Two marks. Say equally distributed and dumb-bell shaped in so many words.",
        "lines": [
          "A vertical section of a monocot leaf shows",
          "three parts:",
          "1. Epidermis   2. Mesophyll   3. Vascular system",
          {
            "text": "1. Epidermis",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "The epidermis is differentiated into the",
          "adaxial epidermis on the upper surface and the",
          "abaxial epidermis on the lower surface.",
          "Both are covered with a cuticle, which reduces",
          "transpiration.",
          "Stomata are equally distributed on both the",
          "surfaces.",
          "Each stoma is bounded by two dumb-bell shaped",
          "guard cells."
        ],
        "why": "A grass leaf stands almost upright, so both faces receive about the same light and neither face is more shaded than the other; there is then no reason to put more stomata on one side, and equal distribution is what gives the leaf its name.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the stomata are mostly on the lower surface. That is the dicot leaf.",
          "Giving bean shaped guard cells. In a monocot they are dumb-bell shaped."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Iso means equal: equal on both surfaces, stomata included."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_bulliform",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Bulliform cells",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Bulliform cells",
        "margin_note": "One mark, four short lines. Give both names and both directions of the movement.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Bulliform cells",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "In grasses, large, empty, colourless cells are",
          "present in the adaxial epidermis along the",
          "veins. These are called bulliform cells or",
          "motor cells.",
          "When they lose water they shrink, and the leaf",
          "rolls inward.",
          "When they take up water they swell, and the",
          "leaf unrolls.",
          "Rolling reduces the surface exposed to the",
          "air, so less water is lost."
        ],
        "why": "These cells are thin walled and hold a lot of water, so they change size more than the cells around them; that uneven shrinking is what bends the leaf, which is why they are also called motor cells.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Placing the bulliform cells in the lower epidermis. They lie in the upper epidermis.",
          "Saying they help in photosynthesis. They are colourless and have no chloroplasts.",
          "Leaving out the second name. Motor cells is also marked."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Empty cells in the upper epidermis: they lose water and the leaf rolls, they take up water and it opens."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_mesophyll",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Mesophyll",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Undifferentiated mesophyll",
        "margin_note": "Two marks and the heart of the answer. Say not differentiated in those words.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "2. Mesophyll",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "The tissue lying between the upper and the",
          "lower epidermis is the mesophyll.",
          "It is not differentiated into palisade",
          "parenchyma and spongy parenchyma. All its",
          "cells are of one kind, more or less rounded,",
          "with intercellular spaces between them.",
          "Because the mesophyll is undifferentiated, the",
          "leaf is called isobilateral.",
          "Every mesophyll cell contains chloroplasts and",
          "takes part in photosynthesis."
        ],
        "why": "With the same light falling on both faces there is no upper half and no lower half to specialise, so one kind of cell serves the whole thickness of the leaf. This is the difference the whole answer is built on.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the mesophyll has spongy parenchyma only. It has neither kind; the cells are all alike.",
          "Copying the dicot leaf answer here. The mesophyll is the point of difference."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "One kind of cell from top to bottom: that is what undifferentiated means."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_vascular",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Vascular system",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Bundles described",
        "margin_note": "Close with the one-line summary. It answers the word isobilateral in the question.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "3. Vascular system",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "The vascular bundles are of similar size,",
          "except in the main veins, where they are",
          "larger.",
          "Each bundle is conjoint, collateral and",
          "closed, and is surrounded by a bundle sheath.",
          "The xylem lies towards the adaxial surface and",
          "the phloem towards the abaxial surface.",
          {
            "text": "Isobilateral: mesophyll not differentiated",
            "style": "boxed"
          }
        ],
        "why": "The veins of a grass leaf run parallel and are nearly equal, so the bundles cut across in a section are nearly equal too. In a dicot leaf the veins branch, so the bundles vary widely in size.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing open for a leaf bundle. It is closed.",
          "Saying the bundles are all exactly equal. They are of similar size except in the main veins."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Parallel veins of one size give bundles of one size."
      },
